rawn 1), totaling 21, which is much better than exposing early for maybe fewer points.\\n\\nHowever, if I pass, I retain the ability to form T1-2-3 concealed later if I draw the necessary tiles, but claiming it now locks it in.\\n\\nWait, but if I pass, someone else might claim the T3 discard. But since the valid actions are only Chi T2 and Pass, that means only I have the opportunity to Chi this T3 (because it's from the previous player, who is to my right? Wait, no\\u2014in Mahjong, the order is usually counter-clockwise. Assuming previous player is to my right, next player to my left.\\n\\nWhen a player discards, the next player (to their left) has first chance to claim, then opposite, then previous. Wait, but in many variants, it's only the next player (immediate left) who can Chi, while Pung/Kong can be claimed by anyone.\\n\\nGiven the problem statement: \\\"Action 'Chi' means forming a Chow using a tile just discarded by the player immediately before you (your left-hand player).\\\" Wait, correction: \\\"by the player immediately before you (your left-hand player)\\\" \\u2014 so the previous player in turn order is the one who discarded, which would be the person to your right? Wait, turn order in Mahjong is typically East->South->West->North, counter-clockwise. So if you're South, previous player is East, next is West.\\n\\nBut regardless, the rule states that Chi can only be done on the discard of the player immediately before you, i.e., your left-hand opponent? Wait, no: \\\"player immediately before you\\\" in turn order would be the one who just played before you, which is to your right.\\n\\nWait, the description says: \\\"Action 'Chi' means forming a Chow using a tile just discarded by the player immediately before you (your left-hand player).\\\"\\n\\nWait, contradiction here. If \\\"immediately before you\\\" in turn order is the player to your right (since turns go counter-clockwise), but it says \\\"(your left-hand player)\\\". Maybe in Chinese Mahjong, the seating is such that the player before you is on your left? Not sure.\\n\\nRegardless, the problem states that the previous player's history includes \\\"Play T3\\\", and valid actions include Chi T2, meaning that I am eligible to Chi that discard. So according to the problem, it's my turn now, previous player discarded T3, and I can choose to Chi.\\n\\nAssuming that passing means letting go of this opportunity. If I pass, no one else can Chi it (since it's only the next player who can Chi, but maybe in this case, since it's specified as valid action, only I have the option).\\n\\nSo if I pass, the tile is gone.\\n\\nTherefore, deciding now whether to take it.\\n\\nAnother consideration: how many outs do I have if I Chi versus not.\\n\\nBut perhaps more importantly, does forming this Chow interfere with potential high-value hands?\\n\\nFor example, Nine Gates is a big hand, but requires 136 of one suit. Not applicable here.\\n\\nThirteen Orphans? No.\\n\\nAll Green? No.\\n\\nBig Four Winds? No.\\n\\nSo likely aiming for something like Pure Straight or similar.\\n\\nGiven that, proceeding with Chi to lock in one Chow seems reasonable, especially since I have the necessary tiles.\\n\\nMoreover, looking at the hand, keeping T1, T2, T3 concealed might not be bad, but converting it now gets a meld out of the way.\\n\\nWait, but if I keep it concealed, I could later expose it if needed, but why delay?\\n\\nHowever, concealed Chows score less? No, in terms of points, exposure doesn't affect the pattern scores directly, except for patterns like Fully Concealed Hand.\\n\\nBut Pure Straight is the same whether concealed or not.\\n\\nThe trade-off is between potential for Fully Concealed Hand (+4) versus possibly completing the hand sooner.\\n\\nGiven that I need at least 8 points, and Pure Straight alone gives 16, which is sufficient, even without concealed bonuses.\\n\\nBut if I can get both Pure Straight and Fully Concealed Hand, that's better. However, by Chi-ing